hi there steve kaufman here to talk once  again about language learning  and today i want to talk about the  importance of our sense of identity our  sense of who we are and i'm going to put  a link in the explanation or comment  section just below my video a link to an  article that appeared in the new yorker  magazine  which shows how  uh in order to try to persuade people  for example to persuade people that  vaccinations are good or to persuade  people of a political point of view it's  very hard to persuade people simply  based on facts  a much bigger part of it is  how these people identify if someone  identifies with a certain political  party or with a particular country then  you can present all the facts you want  about how this political party is bad or  our country did something bad and we  won't believe it we don't want to  believe it because it's crucial to our  sense of identity our sense of who we  are  and there are in this article you'll see  that there are a number of  experiments that have been done that  show  that different efforts to persuade  people of things if it doesn't attack or  if it doesn't deal with this core issue  of their identity then all these  arguments are  useless and in the same article there's  also reference to the fact that for  example in taking tests uh  as a positive affirmation an affirmation  you know i can do it  uh will influence the results or if  girls are convinced that girls are not  good at math and i'm a girl and that's  part of my identity  then i won't do well in the math exam  things like this now  this has real application for language  learning and i've always noticed it  in the book that i wrote almost 10 years  ago i refer to the importance of uh sort  of cultural weightlessness  the more we think we are unique and our  culture is unique and we're sort of tied  up in this whole sense of who we are  the more difficult it is to learn  another language because to learn  another language you have to  want to be one of them you have to  project yourself into that other  community  and i also mentioned a sort of an  anecdote when i was selling lumber in  japan  and we had a problem with our lumber  grades that the japanese felt or didn't  quite match the needs of the market so  we sent a  chief grader with 30 years of experience  working in a small town at the sawmill  we sent him to japan so he could better  understand the needs of the japanese  customers but of course he went there  with his identity of someone who had  graded according to the north american  grading rule for 30 years and he worked  his way up the ladder and this is what  this is who he was so rather than trying  to listen to  the customers how they wanted the lumber  graded he would lecture them on how we  did it  well one evening the customer took us  out to a japanese bar  and particularly in those days if you  went to a japanese bar you got a nice  little girl to sit beside you a little  girl like someone in their 20s cutie  and she would talk with you and tell you  how handsome you were and pour your  drinks and so forth  and of course i was there and i was  speaking japanese and this fellow  couldn't speak japanese and this girl  his girl said to him and do you speak  japanese and he'd had a few drinks and  he said no but i can learn  and that evening he had a great time  and don't read too much into it we all  went home after our few drinks there  but the next day because now all of a  sudden he had softened a bit he was  maybe a little happier to be in japan  felt a little less threatened his he  wasn't clinging to his identity as a  canadian lumber grader  and he started listening  and so it was again an example of this  whole importance of attitude and and our  sense of identity when it comes to  language learning  as i again i will repeat because it  bears repeating language learning is  based on only three things all the rest  doesn't matter it doesn't matter who  your teacher is who your grammar book is  whether you're on link or not on link  what matters is your attitude  you think you can do it you want to do  it  you like doing it the second thing is  time the amount of time you spend that's  going to depend on your attitude and the  third thing is developing that ability  to notice but that also depends on  attitude you have to want to notice  uh and so the sense of identity if  you're prepared  to not cling to your own identity and  i'm english-speaking north american and  therefore i'm not good at languages or  we japanese are so unique or any of  these kinds of things uh where you're  clinging to that identity and you won't  let go of that sort of  um  comfort  of that  what they call it safety blanket  so  identity is a big deal positive  affirmation but meaning it really  thinking that you are someone who can  operate in different cultures  you don't have to be in your own culture  you can enjoy other cultures you can  enjoy interacting with people of a  different culture you can learn another  language you like learning it all of  these kinds of things that relate to  attitude  but but really relate to this idea of  identity so  it's interesting how we as human beings  work we're not that rational  um you know uh  some of our belief systems are very hard  to dislodge  so read the article  i thought it was very interesting and  you'll see that it has application as  well for language learning and i look  forward to your own experiences in this  regard  and i thank you for listening bye for  now
 

